[SPEAKER_01]: Like, say, say things have proliferated mistakenly.

583
00:39:10,633 --> 00:39:18,055
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you want to bring it back to the point that's kind of good enough, close to the point when it doesn't matter what proliferation is.

584
00:39:18,615 --> 00:39:20,435
[SPEAKER_01]: And that point is experience as a whole.

585
00:39:21,416 --> 00:39:30,718
[SPEAKER_01]: But, if you start even picking writing based upon the right instruction, or I'll attend to this, I'll attend to this, but you attend it further down the line, after things have proliferated,

586
00:39:31,989 --> 00:39:32,729
[SPEAKER_01]: It won't work then.

587
00:39:32,829 --> 00:39:33,670
[SPEAKER_01]: It won't take the root.

588
00:39:34,130 --> 00:39:37,371
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, but I'm saying, like, attendant means exactly what I said before.

589
00:39:37,651 --> 00:39:42,854
[SPEAKER_01]: You have to start with experience as a whole, not just pick an object within already a non-mindful experience as a whole.

590
00:39:44,394 --> 00:39:47,456
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you discern the object within the experience as a whole.

591
00:39:47,776 --> 00:39:50,337
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't pick the object on top of the experience as a whole.

592
00:39:51,838 --> 00:39:52,898
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's a different direction.

593
00:39:53,258 --> 00:39:54,219
[SPEAKER_01]: Can you say that again?

594
00:39:54,599 --> 00:39:59,941
[SPEAKER_01]: You discern the object within the experience as a whole that you're mindful of.

595
00:40:00,890 --> 00:40:05,534
[SPEAKER_01]: Within that, then, you discern the object without losing the mindfulness of the experience as a whole.

596
00:40:06,114 --> 00:40:13,660
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's the opposite direction from the direction of how to pick an object, without a basis of experience as a whole.

597
00:40:14,460 --> 00:40:15,941
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you don't pick it, you find it.

598
00:40:17,142 --> 00:40:18,763
[SPEAKER_01]: You step into it, so to speak.

599
00:40:20,985 --> 00:40:27,210
[SPEAKER_00]: So, there's two of those two simultaneous things, a particular general positive, negative one, say?

600
00:40:27,870 --> 00:40:30,172
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, but the order does matter.

601
00:40:31,404 --> 00:40:32,064
[SPEAKER_01]: Because, you know, you...

602
00:40:32,144 --> 00:40:32,705
[SPEAKER_01]: I can have a...

603
00:40:33,125 --> 00:40:34,245
[SPEAKER_01]: I can... like, there you go.

604
00:40:34,305 --> 00:40:35,406
[SPEAKER_01]: I can be...

605
00:40:35,446 --> 00:40:37,886
[SPEAKER_01]: I can be attending to this table and the things on the table.

606
00:40:38,507 --> 00:40:39,547
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's a particular thing.

607
00:40:39,587 --> 00:40:42,188
[SPEAKER_01]: But then, within that, I can be attending on the cup that's on top of the table.

608
00:40:43,068 --> 00:40:46,270
[SPEAKER_01]: So, now, the cup is more particular than the other particular thing.

609
00:40:46,370 --> 00:40:50,671
[SPEAKER_01]: But, really, they're all particular things, because I'm forgetting about myself.

610
00:40:50,691 --> 00:40:52,012
[SPEAKER_00]: Yourself and your situation.

611
00:40:52,112 --> 00:40:53,252
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly.

612
00:40:53,292 --> 00:40:53,492
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

613
00:40:53,893 --> 00:40:56,274
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's why that proliferation methods.

614
00:40:56,374 --> 00:40:57,294
[SPEAKER_01]: You need to find a place

615
00:40:58,427 --> 00:41:01,688
[SPEAKER_01]: Bring it back where you include yourself, which is experience as a whole.

616
00:41:01,848 --> 00:41:02,089
[SPEAKER_01]: Why?

617
00:41:02,109 --> 00:41:09,371
[SPEAKER_01]: Because it's the proliferation that takes that sense of self out, and then leaves it, uh, leaves it intact, no matter what reflection you do.

618
00:41:09,572 --> 00:41:10,712
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, yeah, this cup is impermanent.

619
00:41:10,732 --> 00:41:11,632
[SPEAKER_01]: This cup is mine.

620
00:41:11,712 --> 00:41:13,393
[SPEAKER_01]: It's very dear to me, and so on and so forth.

621
00:41:13,693 --> 00:41:14,493
[SPEAKER_01]: But it's impermanent.

622
00:41:14,534 --> 00:41:15,094
[SPEAKER_01]: It's made of this.

623
00:41:15,134 --> 00:41:15,654
[SPEAKER_01]: It's made of that.

624
00:41:15,934 --> 00:41:17,155
[SPEAKER_01]: You left yourself out again.

625
00:41:17,675 --> 00:41:17,995
Yeah.

626
00:41:18,135 --> 00:41:26,478
[SPEAKER_01]: But if you find yourself in experience as a whole, sitting in this room, thinking about this cup, that's very dear and precious gift that was given to me, which it

627
00:41:28,960 --> 00:41:31,541
[SPEAKER_01]: Then, okay, so you start with yourself as the basis.

628
00:41:32,721 --> 00:41:36,122
[SPEAKER_01]: And within that, you discern the object of their value.

629
00:41:38,663 --> 00:41:40,384
[SPEAKER_01]: And in that direction, reflection can go.

630
00:41:40,664 --> 00:41:43,645
[SPEAKER_01]: But not just go out, forgetting about yourself.

631
00:41:44,745 --> 00:41:47,766
[SPEAKER_01]: That's where experience as a whole, mindfulness of it, needs to come first.

632
00:41:48,166 --> 00:41:53,768
[SPEAKER_00]: So, when we say a situation, it's...

633
00:41:53,788 --> 00:41:54,929
[SPEAKER_01]: It implies you, always.

634
00:41:55,229 --> 00:41:55,629
[SPEAKER_01]: It should.

635
00:41:55,909 --> 00:41:56,309
[SPEAKER_01]: Okay.

636
00:41:56,989 --> 00:41:58,750
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you are always in the situation.

637
00:42:04,442 --> 00:42:12,327
[SPEAKER_01]: And that can be anything as a whole.

638
00:42:12,807 --> 00:42:19,191
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, whatever you are, whatever you're doing, whatever you're thinking, whatever you're feeling, that's part of the experience as a whole that's already there.

639
00:42:20,091 --> 00:42:22,453
[SPEAKER_01]: The presence of the five aggregates, that's the situation.

640
00:42:23,020 --> 00:42:28,102
[SPEAKER_00]: And it's also not a matter of saying, oh, this is the feeling that the perception... No, no, it doesn't matter, no.

641
00:42:28,302 --> 00:42:30,843
[SPEAKER_01]: You can discern that within that situation, then.

642
00:42:31,284 --> 00:42:35,505
[SPEAKER_01]: And then you would actually be discerning that which feeling is.

643
00:42:35,525 --> 00:42:43,389
[SPEAKER_01]: As opposed to just assuming and thinking, then attending upon your assumption of what you think the feeling is.

644
00:42:43,409 --> 00:42:51,092
[SPEAKER_01]: That's I mean, you find those things in the experience, once experience as a whole has been brought up, brought up to light with mindfulness sufficient amount of times.

645
00:42:52,901 --> 00:42:58,463
[SPEAKER_01]: But without that as a basis, you're not actually fishing in the right pond, so to speak.

646
00:42:58,483 --> 00:43:12,147
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, as you think of the words experience as a whole and situation, but I... there have only been maybe a few times where I've sort of been like, oh, this might actually be what that means.

647
00:43:12,287 --> 00:43:12,547
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

648
00:43:12,587 --> 00:43:15,748
[SPEAKER_00]: Otherwise, it's always kind of a vague thing, what that might mean.

649
00:43:15,809 --> 00:43:15,989
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

650
00:43:16,129 --> 00:43:20,230
[SPEAKER_01]: And it is, it is a vague, it will be a vague experience, because it's basically like mind waking up.

651
00:43:21,446 --> 00:43:24,728
[SPEAKER_01]: Mindful being constantly absorbed in things, just waking up.

652
00:43:26,550 --> 00:43:30,512
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why some people, that's why people, like, you know, they come across it.

653
00:43:30,632 --> 00:43:33,694
[SPEAKER_01]: Even people with the wrong view, just experience the whole mindfulness.

654
00:43:34,195 --> 00:43:38,738
[SPEAKER_01]: And the minds are blown away by it, because they're never used to it, that they spiritualize it, they mystify it.

655
00:43:39,338 --> 00:43:46,063
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, you can see that often, hear that certainly, and it's kind of the pinnacle of every teaching in every religion.

656
00:43:46,617 --> 00:43:52,321
[SPEAKER_01]: You just be here now, be mindful, the pure presence, the pristine awareness.

657
00:43:52,722 --> 00:43:55,824
[SPEAKER_01]: You just mystified mindfulness of the experience as a whole.

658
00:43:56,765 --> 00:43:58,966
[SPEAKER_01]: But that in itself means nothing.

659
00:44:00,788 --> 00:44:06,332
[SPEAKER_01]: As you can see by all the different teachers and people that talk lots of stuff, that doesn't actually lead anywhere.

660
00:44:07,248 --> 00:44:08,269
[SPEAKER_01]: Anybody can be mindful.

661
00:44:08,549 --> 00:44:11,191
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why I say, like, you need to know what you're supposed to look for.

662
00:44:11,231 --> 00:44:13,293
[SPEAKER_01]: So, yes, absolutely, mindfulness comes first.

663
00:44:13,313 --> 00:44:14,614
[SPEAKER_01]: You can never have too much mindfulness.

664
00:44:14,894 --> 00:44:16,956
[SPEAKER_01]: But that in itself, it's certainly not the end.

665
00:44:16,976 --> 00:44:19,498
[SPEAKER_01]: That's just the beginning.

666
00:44:19,518 --> 00:44:21,379
[SPEAKER_01]: But for most people, that's where the practice ends.

667
00:44:21,459 --> 00:44:25,162
[SPEAKER_01]: Just spend your rest of your life, just be here now.

668
00:44:26,063 --> 00:44:31,767
[SPEAKER_01]: Mind, whatever, judgment-free, your mind is present, pristine awareness, all those things.

669
00:44:31,807 --> 00:44:32,368
[SPEAKER_01]: Just do that.

670
00:44:32,448 --> 00:44:32,988
[SPEAKER_01]: Just do that.

671
00:44:33,088 --> 00:44:33,629
[SPEAKER_01]: And then what?

672
00:44:34,169 --> 00:44:34,549
[SPEAKER_01]: That's it.

673
00:44:34,649 --> 00:44:35,230
[SPEAKER_01]: Just do that.

674
00:44:36,489 --> 00:44:37,830
[SPEAKER_00]: And it'll work out eventually.

675
00:44:38,130 --> 00:44:38,530
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly.

676
00:44:38,690 --> 00:44:39,210
[SPEAKER_01]: By itself.

677
00:44:39,230 --> 00:44:39,671
[SPEAKER_01]: But it won't.

678
00:44:39,831 --> 00:44:40,171
[SPEAKER_01]: Of course.

679
00:44:40,611 --> 00:44:41,091
[SPEAKER_01]: And it doesn't.

680
00:44:41,351 --> 00:44:44,153
[SPEAKER_01]: Then, you just end up revolving around more sophisticated wrong views.

681
00:44:44,253 --> 00:44:45,354
[SPEAKER_01]: Or not even so sophisticated.

682
00:44:46,194 --> 00:44:48,435
[SPEAKER_01]: The point is, mindfulness is not the end.

683
00:44:48,915 --> 00:44:50,576
[SPEAKER_01]: It's the beginning of your practice.

684
00:44:50,976 --> 00:44:54,518
[SPEAKER_01]: So, yes, start with that pristine awareness, which is a contradiction in terms.

685
00:44:54,558 --> 00:44:55,058
[SPEAKER_01]: Doesn't matter.

686
00:44:55,619 --> 00:44:56,639
[SPEAKER_01]: Call it whatever you want.

687
00:44:57,019 --> 00:44:57,259
[SPEAKER_01]: Do it.

688
00:44:57,380 --> 00:45:02,182
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, within that, start discerning what you're supposed to know, which is what the suttas tell you.

689
00:45:03,483 --> 00:45:06,625
[SPEAKER_01]: But don't use the sutra just to come to that experience and then mystify yourself.

690
00:45:10,428 --> 00:45:12,289
[SPEAKER_01]: Because the feelings need to be understood.

691
00:45:13,309 --> 00:45:14,951
[SPEAKER_01]: The thoughts need to be understood.

692
00:45:15,451 --> 00:45:16,972
[SPEAKER_01]: The perception needs to be understood.

693
00:45:17,792 --> 00:45:22,736
[SPEAKER_00]: How are you going to understand that if you just sort of... You don't see what's surrounding you.

694
00:45:22,756 --> 00:45:23,116
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly.

695
00:45:23,656 --> 00:45:27,759
[SPEAKER_01]: If you're just kind of frozen in this ecstatic... not even ecstatic, just this...

696
00:45:28,433 --> 00:45:34,855
[SPEAKER_01]: Neither here nor there, all around, luminous presence of my pristine awareness, and then what?

697
00:45:35,015 --> 00:45:35,355
[SPEAKER_01]: Great.

698
00:45:40,196 --> 00:45:44,337
[SPEAKER_01]: But if you actually don't forget that you're supposed to understand these things, then it becomes just wrong.

699
00:45:44,417 --> 00:45:45,878
[SPEAKER_01]: This is just mindfulness.

700
00:45:45,898 --> 00:45:47,378
[SPEAKER_00]: Mindfulness of the experience itself.

701
00:45:48,138 --> 00:45:50,419
[SPEAKER_01]: Sure, feels light, feels luminous, feels whatever else.

702
00:45:51,079 --> 00:45:57,941
[SPEAKER_01]: In the comparison to, like, states before, states of non-mindfulness, and when I said people are not mindful as if already dead,

703
00:45:59,201 --> 00:46:00,062
[SPEAKER_01]: That's all true.

704
00:46:00,943 --> 00:46:02,004
[SPEAKER_01]: But that's what mindfulness is.

705
00:46:02,444 --> 00:46:04,306
[SPEAKER_01]: So you kind of wake up to do the work.

706
00:46:05,767 --> 00:46:09,991
[SPEAKER_01]: Not wake up and celebrate that you woke up and you haven't done any work.

707
00:46:11,773 --> 00:46:14,816
[SPEAKER_01]: Which is what most spiritual practices are today.

708
00:46:14,836 --> 00:46:19,240
[SPEAKER_01]: Just grateful, gratitude, ease and whatnot.

709
00:46:19,300 --> 00:46:20,221
[SPEAKER_01]: But no work done.

710
00:46:21,202 --> 00:46:22,363
[SPEAKER_01]: Just we reach this.

711
00:46:22,403 --> 00:46:24,124
[SPEAKER_01]: So, look, we can be mindful.

712
00:46:26,190 --> 00:46:29,712
[SPEAKER_01]: So, no wonder now people try to take the mindfulness and apply it.

713
00:46:30,892 --> 00:46:48,099
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, speaking with a psychiatrist the other day in Kenya, Australia, and they were saying there's a whole movement, and I know there was even in England, this whole movement of applying mindfulness, like, not, not, not in a, not in a, like, a religious context, not in a Buddhist context, just, just the mindfulness for this mindfulness.

714
00:46:48,159 --> 00:46:49,500
[SPEAKER_00]: It's just, yeah, great.

715
00:46:54,294 --> 00:47:05,344
[SPEAKER_01]: Even the NHS in England, National Health, National Health Service, even they started doing courses in mindfulness therapy.

716
00:47:05,544 --> 00:47:06,045
[SPEAKER_01]: Bless them.

717
00:47:07,786 --> 00:47:08,987
[SPEAKER_01]: It's fine.

718
00:47:09,067 --> 00:47:17,755
[SPEAKER_01]: I mean, sure, mindfulness is always better than non-mindfulness, but don't mistake mere mindfulness for understanding of the dhamma and being free from suffering.

719
00:47:19,656 --> 00:47:20,637
[SPEAKER_01]: That's basically my point.

720
00:47:22,245 --> 00:47:23,386
[SPEAKER_01]: If it's on the stage, great.

721
00:47:23,586 --> 00:47:24,766
[SPEAKER_01]: That often is, obviously.

722
00:47:24,826 --> 00:47:26,067
[SPEAKER_01]: And people think it's the goal.

723
00:47:26,167 --> 00:47:26,527
[SPEAKER_01]: That's it.

724
00:47:26,927 --> 00:47:28,128
[SPEAKER_01]: That's the goal of the whole practice.

725
00:47:28,348 --> 00:47:30,329
[SPEAKER_01]: Just be here now, present.

726
00:47:31,590 --> 00:47:34,191
[SPEAKER_01]: You are already now present, whether you want to be or not.

727
00:47:35,092 --> 00:47:37,793
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't have to make any effort to be present, because you have no choice about it.

728
00:47:39,454 --> 00:47:48,558
[SPEAKER_01]: So you can just become aware of it, and then you can either go all mystical, or you can become aware of it, and as soon as, okay, now I have to do the work, because now I'm aware.

729
00:47:49,519 --> 00:47:51,620
[SPEAKER_01]: And then you do the work, and the work is understanding.

730
00:47:52,411 --> 00:47:57,073
[SPEAKER_01]: Investigating the meaning, not leaving anything unturned, as many ways as the Buddha would put it.

731
00:47:59,094 --> 00:48:02,516
[SPEAKER_01]: And when it's understood, then, yeah, you can't help but not be mindful, obviously.

732
00:48:05,277 --> 00:48:14,382
[SPEAKER_00]: I think an important criteria for myself is recognizing if a thought I'm having is a description versus an explanation.

733
00:48:15,022 --> 00:48:16,643
[SPEAKER_01]: Do you know what I mean?

734
00:48:16,663 --> 00:48:16,903
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

735
00:48:16,923 --> 00:48:20,565
[SPEAKER_01]: And see, that can also be determined whether you're mindful of it or not.

736
00:48:22,114 --> 00:48:27,258
[SPEAKER_01]: If you're mindful of it properly, it means you're not losing the base of the experience as a whole, which means then it's a description.

737
00:48:28,038 --> 00:48:31,941
[SPEAKER_01]: But you can have the same thought, but it's baseless, and then it's an explanation.

738
00:48:33,022 --> 00:48:37,044
[SPEAKER_00]: So, when we say explanation, that basically means almost like abstract.

739
00:48:37,825 --> 00:48:42,388
[SPEAKER_01]: Becomes abstract, becomes abstracted from the base that's non-existent.

740
00:48:42,408 --> 00:48:44,490
[SPEAKER_01]: Base being... Mindfulness as a whole.

741
00:48:44,510 --> 00:48:44,990
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly.

742
00:48:46,165 --> 00:48:48,988
[SPEAKER_01]: That's what I was saying, I think, in one of the talks with Tanya.

743
00:48:49,049 --> 00:48:52,753
[SPEAKER_01]: I was telling that abstract is basically your attitude, not necessarily the content.

744
00:48:55,136 --> 00:48:59,822
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you can think something great as an explanation, but it's not.

745
00:48:59,922 --> 00:49:00,943
[SPEAKER_00]: Exactly, exactly.

746
00:49:08,886 --> 00:49:16,528
[SPEAKER_01]: If you were to re-establish the mindfulness and have the same abstract thought, it will cease to be abstract, because now it's actually standing for that which it represents.

747
00:49:17,548 --> 00:49:26,970
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why I say, when Bhikkhu Bodhi, he says in his translations, oh, Paticca Upādāna, when this, this is, the first line, it's just an abstract principle.

748
00:49:28,391 --> 00:49:31,091
[SPEAKER_01]: And then he goes on explaining rest of Paticca Upādāna differently.

749
00:49:31,111 --> 00:49:33,832
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, well, it's an abstract principle only because you don't understand it.

750
00:49:34,943 --> 00:49:35,903
[SPEAKER_01]: Hence, it's abstracted.

751
00:49:38,004 --> 00:49:43,385
[SPEAKER_01]: So, far from dismissing something because it's abstract, that you just tell you that actually you don't understand it.

752
00:49:43,685 --> 00:49:45,686
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you should understand it when it ceases to be abstract.

753
00:49:46,566 --> 00:49:53,008
[SPEAKER_01]: So, when you read all this, when the Buddha describes the states of mind and whatever else, the people usually, oh, this is just abstract or something.

754
00:49:53,348 --> 00:49:55,468
[SPEAKER_01]: It's abstract because you have no clue what he's talking about.

755
00:49:55,508 --> 00:49:58,149
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you have to find out and then it won't be abstract.

756
00:49:58,619 --> 00:50:03,681
[SPEAKER_01]: The element of earth, the casino of earth, the casino of this, the casino of that... For most people, it's just abstract.

757
00:50:03,701 --> 00:50:05,561
[SPEAKER_01]: They have absolutely no idea what they're referred to.

758
00:50:06,862 --> 00:50:10,143
[SPEAKER_01]: But that doesn't mean that the actual content is abstract.

759
00:50:10,163 --> 00:50:15,224
[SPEAKER_01]: It just means their minds are so far abstracted from that, they have no concept of it.

760
00:50:18,305 --> 00:50:27,348
[SPEAKER_00]: So, when we're doing it properly, like in terms of descriptions, if we want to use that word, as opposed to explanations,

761
00:50:28,626 --> 00:50:35,671
[SPEAKER_00]: It means, then, that we have that background, we have awareness of it, and then...

762
00:50:35,691 --> 00:50:37,593
[SPEAKER_01]: Within that, yeah, exactly.

763
00:50:39,874 --> 00:50:41,295
[SPEAKER_00]: And, of course, I can say that.

764
00:50:41,676 --> 00:50:42,717
[SPEAKER_00]: It's easy enough to say.

765
00:50:42,877 --> 00:50:46,980
[SPEAKER_01]: It's easy enough to say, but it's hard to repeat it sufficient amount of times.

766
00:50:47,620 --> 00:50:48,601
[SPEAKER_01]: But it's not impossible.

767
00:50:50,162 --> 00:50:51,283
[SPEAKER_01]: And it's not hard in terms of

768
00:50:52,274 --> 00:50:55,336
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, crap, how can I be mindful of the experiences?

769
00:50:55,456 --> 00:50:57,417
[SPEAKER_01]: Everybody can be mindful of the experience as a whole.

770
00:50:57,457 --> 00:50:59,718
[SPEAKER_01]: Nothing specific, just on the in and out.

771
00:50:59,958 --> 00:51:01,198
[SPEAKER_01]: You can start with that if you want.

