01:03:42,106 --> 01:03:43,067
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, they're subtler.

969
01:03:43,487 --> 01:03:52,734
[SPEAKER_01]: So, basically, as long as there is a determination that's rooted in ignorance, there's going to be the whole... but it's all part of the results in suffering, and so on.

970
01:03:54,215 --> 01:03:57,317
[SPEAKER_01]: And, ultimately, the ultimate determination and realization is consciousness.

971
01:03:58,037 --> 01:04:06,003
[SPEAKER_01]: So, as long as there is presence of things, as long as things are arising, there will be suffering.

972
01:04:06,343 --> 01:04:07,024
[SPEAKER_01]: We can say that.

973
01:04:09,328 --> 01:04:11,870
[SPEAKER_01]: Again, all that means when ignorance is there.

974
01:04:13,351 --> 01:04:29,903
[SPEAKER_01]: Once ignorance is dispelled in regard to nature of manifestation, nature of presence, nature of consciousness, then there's no problem anymore.

975
01:04:29,923 --> 01:04:31,964
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, because there's some similarities you mentioned.

976
01:04:39,008 --> 01:04:43,809
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, yeah, if it leans, if it sits, yeah, you don't don't rest on anything.

977
01:04:43,829 --> 01:04:48,770
[SPEAKER_00]: You use that in regards to contact.

978
01:04:49,391 --> 01:04:53,832
[SPEAKER_00]: Like whatever your contact with has the potential to be devouring.

979
01:04:54,032 --> 01:04:59,573
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, no, every contact is devouring, because contact already means ignorance.

980
01:04:59,733 --> 01:05:00,133
[SPEAKER_00]: Touched.

981
01:05:00,913 --> 01:05:07,975
[SPEAKER_01]: You can only be touched when you're ignorant, which means the fact that contact, even as a pleasant contact, is touching you, it's already devouring.

982
01:05:09,795 --> 01:05:12,137
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's why sensual pleasures are actually painful.

983
01:05:12,397 --> 01:05:13,598
[SPEAKER_00]: Actually painful.

984
01:05:13,818 --> 01:05:18,062
[SPEAKER_00]: Even like the most refined contact, like joy and jhāna.

985
01:05:19,343 --> 01:05:21,384
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, not necessarily, though.

986
01:05:22,105 --> 01:05:22,305
[SPEAKER_01]: No?

987
01:05:22,685 --> 01:05:23,146
[SPEAKER_01]: No, no, no.

988
01:05:23,426 --> 01:05:30,932
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, theoretically, supposedly, say, someone without a right view, but, yeah, you want to replace joy with jhāna.

989
01:05:31,092 --> 01:05:33,594
[SPEAKER_01]: You want to use joy with jhāna to replace joy with sensuality.

990
01:05:34,215 --> 01:05:34,675
[SPEAKER_01]: That's fine.

991
01:05:35,398 --> 01:05:35,739
[SPEAKER_00]: Right.

992
01:05:35,799 --> 01:05:37,423
[SPEAKER_00]: I mean, relatively speaking.

993
01:05:37,623 --> 01:05:38,826
[SPEAKER_01]: Ultimately, sure.

994
01:05:39,246 --> 01:05:43,896
[SPEAKER_01]: Again, if ignorance is there, which can be, even the one who has jhānas,

995
01:05:44,635 --> 01:05:46,557
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, there's still going to be contact.

996
01:05:47,077 --> 01:05:49,920
[SPEAKER_01]: From the ultimate fundamental point of view, you don't want contact at all.

997
01:05:50,320 --> 01:06:01,069
[SPEAKER_01]: However, refining contact in regard to the wholesome states, as opposed to in regard to the unwholesome states, can make you and certainly enable you to see contact, natural contact, clearer and then free yourself from it.

998
01:06:01,349 --> 01:06:01,610
[SPEAKER_01]: Right.

999
01:06:01,790 --> 01:06:04,212
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why, along with jhānas, we need very little instruction.

1000
01:06:04,312 --> 01:06:05,453
[SPEAKER_01]: Kutujana has jhānas.

1001
01:06:05,833 --> 01:06:07,975
[SPEAKER_01]: You've already done 90% of the mind development.

1002
01:06:09,189 --> 01:06:13,031
[SPEAKER_01]: So, now you just need to get together, get a few bits, pieces right, and that's it.

1003
01:06:13,111 --> 01:06:15,533
[SPEAKER_01]: Then, you'll know how to look at the whole thing correctly.

1004
01:06:18,334 --> 01:06:21,536
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, there's there's two other similes for the other ones.

1005
01:06:22,317 --> 01:06:36,705
[SPEAKER_00]: Let's see... How, because should the nutrient mental volition be seen?

1006
01:06:38,869 --> 01:06:43,171
[SPEAKER_00]: Suppose there's a charcoal pit deep within man's height, filled with burning coals.

1007
01:06:43,992 --> 01:06:48,994
[SPEAKER_00]: Man will come along wanting to live, not wanting to die, desiring happiness in the worst of suffering.

1008
01:06:49,754 --> 01:06:53,936
[SPEAKER_00]: Then two strong men will grab him by both arms, drag him towards the charcoal pit.

1009
01:06:54,936 --> 01:06:57,317
[SPEAKER_00]: A man's volition would be to get far away.

1010
01:06:57,878 --> 01:06:59,659
[SPEAKER_00]: His longing would be to get far away.

1011
01:07:00,159 --> 01:07:02,720
[SPEAKER_00]: His wish would be to get far away from the charcoal pit.

1012
01:07:03,320 --> 01:07:04,040
[SPEAKER_00]: For what reason?

1013
01:07:04,181 --> 01:07:07,382
[SPEAKER_00]: Because he knows, I will fall into this charcoal pit, on account of that,

1014
01:07:15,488 --> 01:07:21,372
[SPEAKER_00]: I don't exactly understand the connection there.

1015
01:07:21,432 --> 01:07:25,675
[SPEAKER_00]: Mental volition, I think that's cetanā.

1016
01:07:26,996 --> 01:07:29,297
[SPEAKER_00]: Is the nutriment sankhāra?

1017
01:07:29,337 --> 01:07:31,659
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, cetanā is sankhāra.

1018
01:07:32,600 --> 01:07:32,900
[SPEAKER_01]: Right.

1019
01:07:33,280 --> 01:07:37,303
[SPEAKER_01]: Sankhāra is just the fundamental nature of determination.

1020
01:07:37,383 --> 01:07:39,104
[SPEAKER_01]: But cetanā is already determination.

1021
01:07:39,304 --> 01:07:40,625
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

1022
01:07:41,025 --> 01:07:43,047
[SPEAKER_01]: So, yeah, basically your intentions.

1023
01:07:44,990 --> 01:07:45,991
[SPEAKER_01]: away from sensuality.

1024
01:07:46,011 --> 01:07:48,372
[SPEAKER_01]: It's the same simile you use how to avoid sensuality.

1025
01:07:49,213 --> 01:07:50,434
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, putting your mind...

1026
01:07:50,734 --> 01:07:57,058
[SPEAKER_01]: In thrown into a chunkle, like burning amber, you get a burning amber, and your mind would be everywhere else except wanting to be there.

1027
01:07:58,159 --> 01:08:02,581
[SPEAKER_01]: Which means the mind has to stop wanting you.

1028
01:08:04,042 --> 01:08:11,647
[SPEAKER_01]: Because simply by wanting sensuality, you are still within the domain of sensuality, even if you're never engaging it for the rest of your life.

1029
01:08:12,948 --> 01:08:14,309
[SPEAKER_01]: But you're still feeling it.

1030
01:08:16,487 --> 01:08:18,869
[SPEAKER_00]: So, one should... Not feed it.

1031
01:08:20,150 --> 01:08:21,911
[SPEAKER_01]: See it as the most dangerous thing.

1032
01:08:23,372 --> 01:08:26,795
[SPEAKER_00]: Develop that intention to avoid it and overcome it.

1033
01:08:27,595 --> 01:08:31,058
[SPEAKER_01]: When it's there, develop intention that you are not going there.

1034
01:08:32,319 --> 01:08:33,900
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, you're being taken there.

1035
01:08:33,980 --> 01:08:36,341
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, but no, you want to be everywhere else except there.

1036
01:08:36,742 --> 01:08:38,543
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's enough to not feed it.

1037
01:08:39,184 --> 01:08:43,807
[SPEAKER_01]: Provided you stop engaging in it by body and your senses beforehand.

1038
01:08:44,769 --> 01:08:46,210
[SPEAKER_00]: Otherwise, it's just hypocritical.

1039
01:08:46,670 --> 01:08:49,551
[SPEAKER_01]: Otherwise, you won't actually stand for anything that matters.

1040
01:08:49,631 --> 01:08:49,851
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

1041
01:08:50,691 --> 01:08:52,852
[SPEAKER_00]: It would be abstract.

1042
01:08:52,932 --> 01:08:54,133
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

1043
01:08:54,613 --> 01:08:56,433
[SPEAKER_00]: Divorce from what's actually in there.

1044
01:08:56,453 --> 01:08:56,714
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

1045
01:08:56,774 --> 01:08:57,214
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, exactly.

1046
01:08:57,434 --> 01:08:57,674
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly.

1047
01:08:57,694 --> 01:08:58,494
[SPEAKER_01]: Completely divorced.

1048
01:08:58,534 --> 01:09:05,457
[SPEAKER_00]: It does not apply to... That's why people can think of that, like the danger centrality, but then they go engage in anyway.

1049
01:09:05,517 --> 01:09:05,897
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, yeah.

1050
01:09:06,437 --> 01:09:10,739
[SPEAKER_01]: And people, you can be like monks of 40, 50 years of practicing.

1051
01:09:10,759 --> 01:09:13,560
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it was in the church, and then they saw and go and find a woman.

1052
01:09:15,365 --> 01:09:16,726
[SPEAKER_01]: Because the mind has never left.

1053
01:09:18,147 --> 01:09:18,907
[SPEAKER_00]: Very scary.

1054
01:09:19,948 --> 01:09:23,650
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, I suppose, yeah, there's a possibility.

1055
01:09:23,670 --> 01:09:26,072
[SPEAKER_00]: Do you think that one could fall into such a trap?

1056
01:09:27,213 --> 01:09:28,634
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, the trap is scary, sure.

1057
01:09:28,934 --> 01:09:29,494
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

1058
01:09:29,714 --> 01:09:32,516
[SPEAKER_01]: But the fact is that it's their own doing, basically.

1059
01:09:33,136 --> 01:09:33,437
[SPEAKER_01]: Of course.

1060
01:09:33,457 --> 01:09:37,839
[SPEAKER_01]: If you spend 50 years not freeing yourself from it, well, you have nobody else to blame, except yourself.

1061
01:09:38,900 --> 01:09:40,121
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not like that you were bound

1062
01:09:40,871 --> 01:09:46,175
[SPEAKER_01]: Simply by a chance, and you have absolutely nothing to do about it, you couldn't do anything about it.

1063
01:09:46,415 --> 01:09:46,915
[SPEAKER_01]: No, you can.

1064
01:09:47,436 --> 01:09:49,777
[SPEAKER_01]: You just didn't know what, because you didn't make the effort to find out.

1065
01:09:50,798 --> 01:09:52,780
[SPEAKER_00]: I think a lot... you know the dasa dhamma sutta?

1066
01:09:52,800 --> 01:09:54,321
[SPEAKER_00]: Which one's that?

1067
01:09:54,341 --> 01:09:56,782
[SPEAKER_00]: The ten perceptions of the four.

1068
01:09:57,543 --> 01:10:02,046
[SPEAKER_00]: I always thought about the last one, that like, you know, you know, the perception...

1069
01:10:02,146 --> 01:10:02,847
The end of my life?

1070
01:10:03,347 --> 01:10:05,028
Yeah, that I won't be embarrassed.

1071
01:10:05,108 --> 01:10:09,932
[SPEAKER_00]: I just think, yeah, what if I'm like a... if I make it that part, 30-year monk, and, you know, people ask,

1072
01:10:10,785 --> 01:10:11,665
[SPEAKER_00]: What have you attained?

1073
01:10:13,866 --> 01:10:18,289
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, the last simile is regarding consciousness.

1074
01:10:19,349 --> 01:10:21,890
[SPEAKER_00]: How, because should the nutrient conscious be seen?

1075
01:10:22,030 --> 01:10:29,554
[SPEAKER_00]: Suppose they were to arrest a bandit, a criminal, and bring him before the king, saying, Sire, this man is a bandit, a criminal.

1076
01:10:30,134 --> 01:10:32,055
[SPEAKER_00]: Impose on him whatever punishment you wish.

1077
01:10:32,655 --> 01:10:36,978
[SPEAKER_00]: The king says to them, Go, men, in the morning, strike this man with a hundred spears.

1078
01:10:36,998 --> 01:10:39,099
[SPEAKER_00]: In the morning, they strike him with a hundred spears.

1079
01:10:39,119 --> 01:10:39,779
Then, at noon,

1080
01:10:40,832 --> 01:10:43,897
[SPEAKER_00]: King asks, how is that man still alive?

1081
01:10:44,639 --> 01:10:47,864
Then, you go with me and show him the hundred more spears and so on and so forth.

1082
01:10:48,465 --> 01:10:52,352
What do you think, Bhikkhu, is with that man being struck with 300 more spears?

1083
01:11:14,289 --> 01:11:24,218
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's going back to the idea of, so long as there is consciousness appropriated, there's going to be the opportunity for pain, suffering.

1084
01:11:24,498 --> 01:11:24,739
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

1085
01:11:24,859 --> 01:11:28,622
[SPEAKER_01]: Even the mere presence of things.

1086
01:11:28,662 --> 01:11:28,882
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

1087
01:11:30,003 --> 01:11:33,787
[SPEAKER_01]: Presence hasn't been understood, means things have been appropriated.

1088
01:11:34,788 --> 01:11:40,093
[SPEAKER_01]: And once you start seeing that, that very root, basically, of the experience, as

1089
01:11:41,059 --> 01:11:48,121
[SPEAKER_01]: Being struck by spirit, it will develop full dispassion towards anything else that comes down the line through that appearance of the experience as a whole.

1090
01:11:49,322 --> 01:11:55,944
[SPEAKER_00]: But it's also more than just simply, I think, seeing that, oh, life is generally... Oh, no.

1091
01:11:55,964 --> 01:11:57,685
[SPEAKER_00]: You don't have a right view for any of those things.

1092
01:11:58,265 --> 01:11:59,565
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, yeah.

1093
01:11:59,725 --> 01:12:03,187
[SPEAKER_01]: Most of that, most of the suttas we saw, bhikkhus, means all sotāpannas, at least.

1094
01:12:03,487 --> 01:12:06,328
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, bhikkhu was already assumed.

1095
01:12:06,348 --> 01:12:07,488
[SPEAKER_01]: No, he's already a sotāpanna.

1096
01:12:08,915 --> 01:12:11,276
[SPEAKER_01]: And the ones that haven't, you hear about them, suttas.

1097
01:12:12,776 --> 01:12:14,577
[SPEAKER_01]: The son of the fisherman, they're the other one.

1098
01:12:15,497 --> 01:12:17,518
[SPEAKER_00]: Right, the one with the wrong views.

1099
01:12:17,678 --> 01:12:18,198
[SPEAKER_00]: Exactly.

1100
01:12:20,158 --> 01:12:21,779
[SPEAKER_01]: So, all of this you need the right view for.

1101
01:12:22,639 --> 01:12:28,421
[SPEAKER_01]: Otherwise, yeah, you can go and moan your whole life and think that life's horrible, but that's not enough for you, aren't you?

1102
01:12:28,921 --> 01:12:31,222
[SPEAKER_01]: You have the right view, and then develop dispassion.

1103
01:12:32,922 --> 01:12:33,682
[SPEAKER_00]: What is a view?

1104
01:12:36,142 --> 01:12:38,744
[SPEAKER_01]: View, basically... Like, I could say those things again.

1105
01:12:38,784 --> 01:12:39,424
[SPEAKER_00]: I could say views.

1106
01:12:39,565 --> 01:12:40,545
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes, I viewed.

1107
01:12:40,565 --> 01:12:41,446
[SPEAKER_00]: What was a view?

1108
01:12:42,086 --> 01:12:42,887
[SPEAKER_01]: What do you think a view is?

1109
01:12:43,788 --> 01:12:44,288
[SPEAKER_01]: Ask yourself.

1110
01:12:44,408 --> 01:12:44,988
[SPEAKER_01]: What is a view?

1111
01:12:45,869 --> 01:12:47,230
[SPEAKER_00]: Do you want me to tell you or just ask me?

1112
01:12:47,250 --> 01:12:47,770
[SPEAKER_00]: Describe it.

1113
01:12:48,511 --> 01:12:49,812
[SPEAKER_01]: How would you describe it to yourself?

1114
01:12:51,593 --> 01:12:52,173
[SPEAKER_01]: Just any.

1115
01:12:52,254 --> 01:12:54,935
[SPEAKER_01]: Don't worry about any standard definition.

1116
01:12:54,955 --> 01:12:56,216
[SPEAKER_01]: Just describe it whichever way.

1117
01:12:58,118 --> 01:13:01,300
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, there is... however experienced it might be.

1118
01:13:01,700 --> 01:13:02,021
It may...

1119
01:13:02,061 --> 01:13:03,522
I may understand, but I may not.

1120
01:13:04,823 --> 01:13:05,403
Whatever...

1121
01:13:06,852 --> 01:13:14,738
[SPEAKER_00]: My understanding of it is, whether it's wrong or right, be a view, but then the confusion comes of... you go beyond view at some point.

1122
01:13:15,199 --> 01:13:16,080
[SPEAKER_00]: How could that be?

1123
01:13:16,380 --> 01:13:17,221
[SPEAKER_01]: No, but what is a view?

1124
01:13:17,641 --> 01:13:18,421
[SPEAKER_01]: The nature of a view?

1125
01:13:18,682 --> 01:13:20,503
[SPEAKER_01]: Doesn't matter good or bad, right or wrong.

1126
01:13:21,204 --> 01:13:21,464
[SPEAKER_01]: View.

1127
01:13:23,446 --> 01:13:26,588
[SPEAKER_01]: What is, what is, what is... How would you describe a view?

1128
01:13:28,970 --> 01:13:29,370
[SPEAKER_01]: How would I?

1129
01:13:29,651 --> 01:13:31,352
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, how is it in the nature of a view?

1130
01:13:31,452 --> 01:13:32,433
[SPEAKER_01]: How would you define?

1131
01:13:43,058 --> 01:13:45,760
[SPEAKER_01]: Would you call it a direction?

1132
01:13:48,962 --> 01:13:49,822
[SPEAKER_01]: View of something?

1133
01:13:51,123 --> 01:13:52,264
[SPEAKER_01]: Point of view?

1134
01:13:52,864 --> 01:13:53,885
[SPEAKER_01]: Coming from something?

1135
01:13:55,986 --> 01:13:56,967
[SPEAKER_01]: Centered in something?

1136
01:13:58,348 --> 01:13:59,469
[SPEAKER_01]: Something is being viewed?

1137
01:13:59,489 --> 01:14:01,270
[SPEAKER_01]: How would you describe it?

1138
01:14:02,731 --> 01:14:04,672
[SPEAKER_00]: I don't mean in terms like I'm viewing the cup.

1139
01:14:05,653 --> 01:14:06,193
[SPEAKER_01]: Even that!

1140
01:14:06,413 --> 01:14:07,154
[SPEAKER_01]: Viewing the cup.

1141
01:14:07,194 --> 01:14:08,314
[SPEAKER_01]: Just view in general.

1142
01:14:08,515 --> 01:14:11,757
[SPEAKER_01]: I'm not talking about you understanding what I'm viewing right here, right now.

1143
01:14:11,937 --> 01:14:12,117
[SPEAKER_01]: Just

1144
01:14:13,098 --> 01:14:13,979
[SPEAKER_01]: Read the word view.

1145
01:14:14,659 --> 01:14:15,260
[SPEAKER_01]: What does it mean?

1146
01:14:18,342 --> 01:14:18,762
[SPEAKER_01]: Any view.

1147
01:14:19,943 --> 01:14:21,704
[SPEAKER_01]: Looking with your eyes, view through the window.

1148
01:14:22,205 --> 01:14:22,705
[SPEAKER_01]: What does it mean?

1149
01:14:25,847 --> 01:14:26,508
[SPEAKER_01]: Seeing something.

1150
01:14:26,988 --> 01:14:27,569
[SPEAKER_01]: Seeing something.

1151
01:14:28,669 --> 01:14:30,391
[SPEAKER_01]: And there is already the direction implied.

1152
01:14:31,511 --> 01:14:33,413
[SPEAKER_01]: From here, you're seeing something there.

1153
01:14:33,833 --> 01:14:34,033
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

1154
01:14:34,494 --> 01:14:35,815
[SPEAKER_01]: And same goes for the mental view.

1155
01:14:35,835 --> 01:14:41,799
[SPEAKER_01]: It's just the right view is the view that's not rooted in unwholesome, not rooted in assumption.

1156
01:14:42,793 --> 01:14:47,495
[SPEAKER_01]: So then, it's still a direction.

1157
01:14:47,515 --> 01:14:49,135
[SPEAKER_00]: The mind towards phenomena.

1158
01:14:49,555 --> 01:14:49,975
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly.

1159
01:14:51,076 --> 01:14:58,298
[SPEAKER_01]: Or, well, we can say consciousness, but obviously then consciousness would stand for something, say, of the mind towards the phenomena.

1160
01:14:58,458 --> 01:15:02,259
[SPEAKER_01]: Because consciousness is out there with phenomena more than it's with your mind.

1161
01:15:02,800 --> 01:15:03,520
[SPEAKER_01]: So the point is,

1162
01:15:04,558 --> 01:15:11,564
[SPEAKER_01]: You have the wrong view, obviously, a view that's not even aware of itself as a view, and then you get the right view, the view that looks at things as they are.

1163
01:15:12,005 --> 01:15:20,032
[SPEAKER_01]: It still looks at them as they are, but an arahant, done that so much, he doesn't even look at them as they are, because that's what they are.

1164
01:15:21,674 --> 01:15:22,755
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you've got to be on the view.

1165
01:15:23,916 --> 01:15:25,937
[SPEAKER_01]: The view is not required.

1166
01:15:25,957 --> 01:15:29,681
[SPEAKER_01]: His look at things rightly, it's not required, because what he has is things rightly.

1167
01:15:33,302 --> 01:15:34,783
[SPEAKER_00]: The view is like a tool.

1168
01:15:35,204 --> 01:15:35,464
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

1169
01:15:35,484 --> 01:15:42,749
[SPEAKER_01]: So, think about, like, when when I say, you know, you set up your mindfulness of your experience at home and then you apply a reflection correctly based on instruction.

1170
01:15:42,789 --> 01:15:45,651
[SPEAKER_01]: All of that implies already the direction of the view.

1171
01:15:46,012 --> 01:15:50,235
[SPEAKER_01]: Then, you can be refining your efforts to the point where you just have the right view.

1172
01:15:50,515 --> 01:15:51,736
[SPEAKER_01]: So, zero effort required.

1173
01:15:51,836 --> 01:15:52,777
[SPEAKER_01]: All you have to do is look.

1174
01:15:53,415 --> 01:15:56,137
[SPEAKER_01]: Because how you look is the right way to look.

1175
01:15:56,457 --> 01:16:01,982
[SPEAKER_01]: And you don't need... No effort, no secular explanations, no setup, no descriptions.

1176
01:16:02,102 --> 01:16:05,384
[SPEAKER_01]: You just look at it, and you look at it rightly, because that's how you train your mind.

1177
01:16:05,404 --> 01:16:07,246
[SPEAKER_01]: You look at it rightly.

1178
01:16:07,266 --> 01:16:13,371
[SPEAKER_01]: Arahant can stop doing that as well, because he can't even conceive wrong anymore, whether he looks or not.

1179
01:16:13,811 --> 01:16:15,112
[SPEAKER_01]: Thus, he is going beyond view.

1180
01:16:15,828 --> 01:16:20,312
[SPEAKER_00]: Could we call it then, like, more in the verb, like, right viewing or wrong viewing?

1181
01:16:20,332 --> 01:16:20,632
[SPEAKER_00]: Sure.

1182
01:16:20,872 --> 01:16:21,112
[SPEAKER_00]: Sure.

1183
01:16:21,272 --> 01:16:22,033
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

1184
01:16:22,053 --> 01:16:27,518
[SPEAKER_01]: The reason why they call it right view, as a noun, is just because you kind of talk about the nature of viewing.

1185
01:16:27,698 --> 01:16:29,599
[SPEAKER_01]: But there's absolutely no view without viewing.

1186
01:16:29,960 --> 01:16:30,240
[SPEAKER_01]: Right.

1187
01:16:30,620 --> 01:16:30,860
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

1188
01:16:31,121 --> 01:16:31,341
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

1189
01:16:34,263 --> 01:16:36,085
[SPEAKER_01]: So, literally, viewing, seeing.

1190
01:16:38,386 --> 01:16:38,867
[SPEAKER_01]: You can do that.

1191
01:16:38,927 --> 01:16:39,607
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's effortless.

1192
01:16:40,308 --> 01:16:42,570
[SPEAKER_01]: That's very, very, like, zero effort required.

1193
01:16:43,723 --> 01:16:48,086
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if you have to have a view, it may or may not be corresponding to what is.

1194
01:16:48,566 --> 01:16:49,987
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, that means that you don't have the right view.

1195
01:16:50,507 --> 01:16:52,168
[SPEAKER_01]: But even the wrong view is kind of effortless.

1196
01:16:52,548 --> 01:16:52,768
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

1197
01:16:53,168 --> 01:16:55,250
[SPEAKER_01]: Just... you don't have to make any further effort.

1198
01:16:55,630 --> 01:17:00,052
[SPEAKER_01]: Now, you have to make loads of effort to undo the wrong view, but when you do so, what you have is the right view.

1199
01:17:00,873 --> 01:17:01,653
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's all that matters.

1200
01:17:02,414 --> 01:17:08,237
[SPEAKER_01]: Because then, he doesn't have to make any further effort except just keeping that view inside, not forgetting about it.

1201
01:17:10,421 --> 01:17:13,806
[SPEAKER_00]: Is it possible for the children to even recognize their wrong views?

1202
01:17:14,427 --> 01:17:14,968
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, yes.

1203
01:17:15,308 --> 01:17:16,650
[SPEAKER_01]: When they do, they become so advanced.

1204
01:17:17,111 --> 01:17:17,371
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh.

1205
01:17:17,632 --> 01:17:18,894
[SPEAKER_00]: So, not until that point?

1206
01:17:19,695 --> 01:17:21,097
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, maybe to a degree.

1207
01:17:21,942 --> 01:17:23,403
[SPEAKER_00]: That would be like a very coarse one.

1208
01:17:23,443 --> 01:17:25,224
[SPEAKER_01]: Because it's not like putujana-vumbh, sotāpanna.

1209
01:17:25,244 --> 01:17:29,366
