[SPEAKER_01]: No, not quite yet.

1221
01:18:03,865 --> 01:18:05,346
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, is he free from sensuality?

1222
01:18:05,606 --> 01:18:06,306
[SPEAKER_01]: No, not quite yet.

1223
01:18:06,646 --> 01:18:07,407
[SPEAKER_01]: But he has the right view?

1224
01:18:07,427 --> 01:18:08,167
[SPEAKER_01]: He has the right view.

1225
01:18:11,778 --> 01:18:13,220
[SPEAKER_01]: Sometimes he doesn't even talk about sakadāgāmī.

1226
01:18:13,240 --> 01:18:16,783
[SPEAKER_01]: Sometimes it's just three mouths, depending on who he sees as relevant.

1227
01:18:16,843 --> 01:18:17,484
[SPEAKER_01]: It doesn't matter.

1228
01:18:19,146 --> 01:18:23,330
[SPEAKER_01]: The point is, you have to keep trying to understand where the wrong view is.

1229
01:18:23,690 --> 01:18:24,651
[SPEAKER_01]: That's how you get the right view.

1230
01:18:25,132 --> 01:18:27,975
[SPEAKER_01]: Or, you have to keep trying to understand where the right view is.

1231
01:18:27,995 --> 01:18:29,076
[SPEAKER_01]: That's how you get the right view.

1232
01:18:30,186 --> 01:18:34,348
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you can get closer to understanding what you're trying to understand.

1233
01:18:34,868 --> 01:18:41,391
[SPEAKER_01]: Still not quite understand, so you're still kind of a pratījana, but you're definitely closer to understanding than a pratījana who never made the effort to understand at all.

1234
01:18:44,133 --> 01:18:47,754
[SPEAKER_01]: It's like a gradual shoving of the sea, as the sīla says.

1235
